# Portfolio Project Guidelines & Rules for Cline

## Tech Stack & Standards (2026)
- Meta-Framework: Next.js 15 (App Router, Server Components by default)
- Core Library: React 19 (Use React Compiler patterns, avoid manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` unless necessary)
- Language: TypeScript 5+ (Strict mode enabled, no `any`)
- Styling: Tailwind CSS v4 + Shadcn UI
- Motion: `motion` (formerly Framer Motion)
- Content: MDX via Velite or Contentlayer2

## Architecture & Code Guidelines

### 1. Component Rules
- Default to React Server Components (RSC) unless interactivity (hooks, state, event listeners) is strictly required.
- Add `'use client'` strictly at the top of client component files.
- Keep components small, modular, and single-purpose in `src/components/`.
- Use explicit TypeScript interfaces/types for all component props.

### 2. Styling Rules
- Use Tailwind CSS v4 inline classes and utility functions (`cn()` from `clsx` and `tailwind-merge`).
- Do not create custom CSS files or heavy inline `style={}` objects when Tailwind utilities exist.
- Support dark mode out of the box using CSS variables and Tailwind classes.

### 3. File & Directory Structure
src/
├── app/              # Next.js App Router (pages, layouts, API routes)
├── components/       # Reusable UI components
│   ├── ui/           # Primitive design system components (Shadcn)
│   └── showcases/    # Project case studies & complex interactive components
├── content/          # MDX files for case studies and post-mortems
├── lib/              # Utility functions, API helpers, GraphQL queries
└── types/            # Global TypeScript definitions

### 4. Quality & Execution Workflow
- Always run type checking (`npx tsc --noEmit`) and linter checks after building or refactoring components.
- Do not write legacy or deprecated code (e.g., Pages Router patterns, legacy Framer Motion imports, outdated Tailwind syntax).
- Ensure high performance: optimize images via `next/image`, avoid heavy bundle sizes, and keep client-side JS minimal.

## 5. Ponytail Minimalism Rules
Before writing any code or introducing new files, evaluate the task in this exact order:
1. Necessity: Does this functionality actually need to exist, or is it speculative?
2. Native First: Can standard browser APIs, native HTML elements, or core runtime utilities solve this without new packages?
3. Code Reuse: Does the current codebase already have a component, utility, or helper that solves this?
4. Minimal Build: If new code is mandatory, write the minimum viable lines. Do not add wrappers, premature abstractions, or extra config.

Note: Never strip security boundaries, error handling, accessibility, or type safety for brevity.

## Agent Behavior Rules
- Be concise and focus on writing production-ready, modular code.
- Always check existing file structures before creating new files to prevent duplicate implementations.
- If a task involves state management or client interactivity, clearly separate data-fetching logic into Server Components or Server Actions.